  ],
  "slug": "optimal-transport-flow-matching-ea35",
  "summary": "New probes show protein language model and foundation model performance depends on invariances, patterning rules and dataset composition rather than learned structure.",
  "synthesis": "The items converge on stress-testing what protein representations actually capture, rather than reporting aggregate performance. Contact maps from protein language models collapse under bijective amino-acid relabeling, unlike Potts and inverse-covariance methods. ESM2 and ESMFold are tested against binary-patterning rules in four-helix bundles using synthetic libraries. OneProt allosteric versus orthosteric site classification is dominated by dataset properties over encoder architecture. Alongside these, PULSE introduces paired-unit local steering for protein design, IFACE defines a symmetric geometric-chemical surface distance, and local magnitude reduces B-factor factorization cost from O(N^3) to O(Nk^3). The previous framing around ligand-pose benchmarks is not supported by these items; the current thread is representation validity and dataset-driven separability.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-15"
}
